  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, thanks for reporting this. I checked and the second floor printer (BL-PR-FL2) is offline on our side too, so the problem is with the printer itself, not your computer. Could you or someone near it try these steps? 1) Look at the printer's screen for any error, like a paper jam, empty paper tray, low toner or an open door, and clear it. 2) Turn the printer off, wait 30 seconds, and turn it back on. 3) Check that the network cable on the back is plugged in firmly and the little light next to it is on. Then reply and tell me what the screen showed and whether it's printing again. If it's still offline after the restart, I'll pass it to our next-level team. Until then, you can print to another printer in the building. In Settings, go to Bluetooth & devices, then Printers & scanners, then Add device, and pick a nearby printer. If it isn't listed, choose 'Add manually' and type the name from the label on that printer (for example BL-PR-FL1).",
  "internal_note": "Sender: James O'Connor (user_id 8, Operations, active account, device BL-DT-0108). I checked BL-PR-FL2: online=false, last check-in 2026-10-09T09:28:54Z, no warnings. Following KB-022, the fault is on the printer, not the user's machine. I asked the user to check the printer's screen for errors, power cycle it for 30s and check the network cable and link light. I also gave him KB-024 steps to add another printer for now. Next step: once he confirms the power cycle, run check_device_status on BL-PR-FL2 again. If it's still offline with no error shown, escalate to Tier 2 per KB-022 (possible network or hardware fault). No action tools used.",
